@page "/"

<PageTitle>Duniverse</PageTitle>

<div class="hero" style="background-image: linear-gradient(rgba(23, 19, 16, 0.82), rgba(23, 19, 16, 0.88)), url('/images/main-menu-background.jpg');">
    <h1>Welcome to the Duniverse</h1>
    <p class="tagline">
        An unofficial encyclopedia of Frank Herbert's Dune - a story of desert planets,
        prophecy, empire, and ecology that reshaped what science fiction could be.
    </p>

<section class="home-section">
    <h2>Why Dune Matters</h2>
    <p>
        Published in 1965, <em>Dune</em> is widely considered one of the most influential science
        fiction novels ever written. Rather than unbelievable space adventures, Frank Herbert built 
        a story around ecology, religion, resource scarcity, and the danger of charismatic
        leaders - a desert planet whose only precious resource, the spice melange, made it
        the center of an entire empire's politics. That premise - a single vital resource
        controlling the fate of civilizations - gave science fiction a new kind of epic:
        one about environments and power as much as heroes and villains.
    </p>
    <p>
        Its fingerprints are all over the science fiction that followed its inception. Critics have
        drawn many parallels between Arrakis and Tatooine, and between Paul Atreides' rise and
        the "chosen one" arc George Lucas would later give Luke Skywalker in <em>Star Wars</em> -
        Dune is frequently cited among the works that pioneered that generation of space epics.
        Herbert himself was more interested in warning against messiahs than creating one: Paul's
        story is a hero's journey deliberately built to make readers uneasy about following heroes
        at all.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="home-section">
    <h2>About Frank Herbert</h2>
    <p>
        Frank Herbert (1920-1986) was an American journalist and science fiction author.
        Before <em>Dune</em> made him famous, he worked for years as a newspaper reporter and
        editor - a background that shows in the novel's dense, research-driven world-building.
        He would go on to write five sequels to <em>Dune</em> himself (<em>Dune Messiah</em>,
        <em>Children of Dune</em>, <em>God Emperor of Dune</em>, <em>Heretics of Dune</em>, and
        <em>Chapterhouse: Dune</em>), expanding the saga across ten thousand years of future
        history before his death in 1986.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="home-section">
    <h2>How Dune Was Born</h2>
    <p>
        The seed of <em>Dune</em> was, quite literally, sand. In the late 1950s, Herbert set out
        to write a magazine article about a U.S. Department of Agriculture project using
        grasses to stabilize the shifting sand dunes along the Oregon coast. The article was
        never finished, but the research stuck with him: the idea of sand as a force that could
        swallow roads, towns, and rivers took root and grew, over the next six years, into an
        entire desert planet, its ecology, its religion, and the people who had learned to
        survive it.
    </p>
    <p>
        The finished novel was serialized in <em>Analog Science Fiction</em> magazine before
        being turned down by roughly twenty publishers, who considered it too long and too
        strange for the market. It finally found a home in 1965 with Chilton Books - a
        publisher best known for auto repair manuals. <em>Dune</em> went on to win the Hugo and
        the inaugural Nebula Award, and remains one of the best-selling science fiction novels
        of all time.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="home-section">
    <h2>In Frank Herbert's Own Words</h2>
    <p>
        Beyond the novel, Herbert spoke and wrote at length about the real dangers he saw in
        charismatic leaders, unchecked power, and technology used without thought - the same
        warnings <em>Dune</em> was built to dramatize.
    </p>
    <div class="quote-grid">
        @foreach (var quote in AuthorQuotes)
        {
            <div class="quote-card">
                <p class="quote-text">"@quote.Text"</p>
                <span class="quote-author">- @quote.Source</span>
            </div>
        }
    </div>
</section>

</div>

@code {
    private record AuthorQuote(string Text, string Source);

    private static readonly List<AuthorQuote> AuthorQuotes = new()
    {
        new("Don't give over all of your critical faculties to people in power, no matter how admirable those people may appear to be.", "Frank Herbert in Dune Genesis, Omni magazine, 1980"),
        new("People tend to give over every decision-making capacity to any leader who can wrap himself in the myth fabric of the society.", "Frank Herbert in Dune Genesis, Omni magazine, 1980"),
        new("Dune was aimed at this whole idea of the infallible leader because my view of history says mistakes made by a leader (or made in a leader's name) are amplified by the numbers who follow without question.", "Frank Herbert in Dune Genesis, Omni magazine, 1980"),
        new("Heroes are painful, superheroes are a catastrophe. The mistakes of superheroes involve too many of us in disaster.", "Frank Herbert in Dune Genesis, Omni magazine, 1980"),
        new("It is demonstrable that power structures tend to attract people who want power for power's sake.", "Frank Herbert in Dune Genesis, Omni magazine, 1980"),
        new("Technology is both a tool for helping humans and for destroying them. This is the paradox of our times which we're compelled to face.", "Frank Herbert in Conversations in Port Townsend, interview with Tim O'Reilly, 1983"),
    };
}
